<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#magyarisation">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magyarisation</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Magyarization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Magyarisation was the policy of spreading the Hungarian language and identity among the non-Hungarian peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, pursued especially after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a>. Measures such as the Apponyi school laws of 1907 required teaching in Hungarian, and many Slovak, Romanian and Serb cultural bodies were restricted or closed. It deepened the national tensions that helped break up the kingdom in 1918–1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magyarisation.jpg" alt="Magyarisation" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Authors: Károly Kocsis, Patrik Tátrai, Norbert Agárdi, Dániel Balizs, András Bognár, Zsolt Bottlik, Anikó Kovács, Árpád E. Varga … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Hungary_-_Ethnic_Map_-_1910.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Smaller ethnic groups had been assimilated into a common Hungarian culture throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, among them Turkic Kabars, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, and some Romanian knezes ennobled by the crown took on the Hungarian language and the Catholic faith. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> remained the language of administration until 1784, and again from 1790 to 1844. Joseph II's attempt to replace it with German provoked a national awakening among the lesser Hungarian nobility. Magyarization as a social policy began in the 1830s, when Hungarian started to replace Latin and German in education. In the early 1840s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth">Lajos Kossuth</a> urged the country to hurry to Magyarize the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, and wrote in 1842 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> must have one language, Hungarian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The term usually refers to the policies of Austro-Hungarian Transleithania after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a>, especially after Count Menyhért Lónyay became prime minister in 1871. It rested on classical liberal ideas of individual citizenship and civic nationalism. The Nationalities Act of 1868 declared all citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> one indivisible political nation with equal rights, yet in practice Hungarian was almost the only language of administration, the courts and higher education. Leaders of the Romanian, Serb and Slovak minorities sought territorial autonomy, which Hungarian politicians, remembering the revolution of 1848, saw as dismemberment of the kingdom.</p><p>From the Primary Education Act of 1879 and the Secondary Education Act of 1883 the state worked to reduce the use of non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> languages in schools. All Slovak secondary schools were closed in 1874 and the Matica slovenská in 1875, and a law of 1897 required that village names be Hungarian. Hungarian names replaced others, especially in towns. At Černová in 1907, 15 people were killed and 52 injured in a shooting; whether this shows violent Magyarization is disputed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, Slovaks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenians">Ruthenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> perceived Magyarization as cultural aggression or discrimination, above all where they formed the local majority. Its results were uneven: between 1880 and 1910 the share of the population speaking Hungarian as a first language rose from 46.6 to 54.5 per cent, mostly in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the educated middle classes, while rural and peripheral populations hardly changed, and the 1910 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> found that about 87 per cent of the minorities could not speak Hungarian. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> adopted Hungarian as their main language. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> about 10.4 per cent of the population of post-Trianon Hungary still spoke a minority language as its mother tongue in 1920.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Magyarization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Policies of <b>Magyarisation</b>, such as the Apponyi school laws of 1907, angered Slovaks, Romanians and Serbs.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hungary-1867-1918">Kingdom of Hungary (1867-1918)</a></li><li><blockquote>As Hungary asserted its independence in spring 1848, Serbs of southern Hungary feared <b>Magyarisation</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/serbian-vojvodina">Serbian Vojvodina</a></li></ul></section>
